Description: CVE-2026-28904 - [JSC] Make RegExp tolerant against excessive stress
 JavaScriptCore's Yarr regular-expression engine had three excessive-stress
 issues, one of which was a critical memory-safety defect (CWE-119):
 .
 1. AssemblerBuffer had no guard against generating extremely large (~4GB) JIT
    code. The capacity growth and index arithmetic could overflow a 32-bit
    unsigned, leading to an out-of-bounds buffer. This is fixed by using
    Checked<unsigned> arithmetic in AssemblerDataImpl::grow() and
    AssemblerBuffer::putIntegral(), and by widening the bounds comparisons in
    isAvailable()/putIntegralUnchecked() to 64-bit.
 2. A RegExp capture-count limit (aligned to V8's value, (1 << 16) / 2) is
    added so that patterns with an excessive number of capturing groups fail
    parsing with a "too many captures" SyntaxError instead of consuming
    unbounded resources.
 3. A RegExp frame-size limit is added: Yarr call-frame offset accounting now
    uses overflow-checked arithmetic (CheckedUint32) in setupAlternativeOffsets/
    setupDisjunctionOffsets, and parsing fails with a "too many frame slots for
    state" SyntaxError when the frame size would exceed the unsigned range.
 .
 This backport adapts the upstream change to webkitgtk 2.50.6: the drifted
 no-argument atomParenthesesSubpatternBegin() overload present in 2.50.6 is
 removed consistently across YarrParser.h, YarrPattern.cpp and
 YarrSyntaxChecker.cpp, and the frame-size overflow guards are applied to the
 2.50.6 layout of setupAlternativeOffsets/setupDisjunctionOffsets.
 Upstream LayoutTests/JSTests expectation updates for files absent in 2.50.6
 (regexp-bol-optimize-out-of-stack.js, stack-overflow-regexp*) are omitted;
 new JSTests/stress regression tests are retained.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/commit/7663d811d06c32554470828470d3c1764144c397
Bug: https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=309601
Author: Yusuke Suzuki <ysuzuki@apple.com>
Last-Update: 2026-09-17
