 ================
From f23ffb5a845006c8efbdf8a18a15f69a617fec00 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chris Dumez <cdumez@apple.com>
Date: Wed, 1 Jul 2026 17:08:24 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Compromised Web Content can use a navigation-response
 Download policy on a data: URL to write attacker-controlled bytes to disk
 https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=315004 rdar://177019828

Reviewed by Basuke Suzuki.

A compromised Web Content process can cause the UI process to write attacker-chosen
bytes to disk with no user interaction. Refuse PolicyAction::Download for data: URLs
in WebPageProxy::receivedNavigationResponsePolicyDecision unless the navigation was
driven by the API client, and extend disallowDataRequest() to the Download branch
in DocumentLoader.

* Source/WebCore/loader/DocumentLoader.cpp:
(WebCore::DocumentLoader::continueAfterContentPolicy):
* Source/WebKit/UIProcess/API/APINavigation.h:
(API::Navigation::isFromAPIClientRequest const):
* Source/WebKit/UIProcess/WebPageProxy.cpp:
(WebKit::WebPageProxy::receivedNavigationResponsePolicyDecision):

Originally-landed-as: 305413.929@safari-7624-branch (8aeb259135a8). rdar://181074672
Canonical link: https://commits.webkit.org/316344@main

Debian bookworm backport: rebased onto webkit2gtk 2.50.6. This is the fix
for CVE-2026-43701 (WSA-2026-0004, bug 315004). APINavigation.h in 2.50.6
has no NODELETE annotations, so isFromAPIClientRequest() is inserted next
to isRequestFromClientOrUserInput(); m_requestIsFromClientInput already
exists. DocumentLoader calls protectedFrameLoader(), which is the spelling
in both 2.50.6 and the webkitgtk 2.52.5 copy of this fix. protect() is not
in 2.50.6.

diff --git a/Source/WebCore/loader/DocumentLoader.cpp b/Source/WebCore/loader/DocumentLoader.cpp
index 0901eb82..3dd9afa2 100644
--- a/Source/WebCore/loader/DocumentLoader.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebCore/loader/DocumentLoader.cpp
@@ -1175,6 +1175,16 @@ void DocumentLoader::continueAfterContentPolicy(PolicyAction policy)
             return;
         }
 
+        // Defense-in-depth: refuse to download a data: URL through a top-frame navigation that
+        // wasn't initiated by the user or the API client, mirroring the existing check in the
+        // PolicyAction::Use branch. The primary defense lives in the UI process; this guards
+        // ports / future flows that don't share that boundary.
+        if (disallowDataRequest()) {
+            protectedFrameLoader()->policyChecker().cannotShowMIMEType(m_response);
+            stopLoadingForPolicyChange();
+            return;
+        }
+
         if (RefPtr mainResourceLoader = this->mainResourceLoader())
             InspectorInstrumentation::continueWithPolicyDownload(*frame, *mainResourceLoader->identifier(), *this, m_response);
 
diff --git a/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/API/APINavigation.h b/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/API/APINavigation.h
index a537700c..46d1cf98 100644
--- a/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/API/APINavigation.h
+++ b/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/API/APINavigation.h
@@ -130,6 +130,7 @@ public:
 
     bool wasUserInitiated() const { return m_lastNavigationAction && !!m_lastNavigationAction->userGestureTokenIdentifier; }
     bool isRequestFromClientOrUserInput() const;
+    bool isFromAPIClientRequest() const { return m_requestIsFromClientInput; }
     void markRequestAsFromClientInput();
     void markAsFromLoadData() { m_isFromLoadData = true; }
     bool isFromLoadData() const { return m_isFromLoadData; }
diff --git a/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/WebPageProxy.cpp b/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/WebPageProxy.cpp
index c18ae86e..ef91c614 100644
--- a/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/WebPageProxy.cpp
+++ b/Source/WebKit/UIProcess/WebPageProxy.cpp
@@ -5138,6 +5138,19 @@ void WebPageProxy::receivedNavigationResponsePolicyDecision(WebCore::PolicyActio
     if (!hasRunningProcess())
         return completionHandler(PolicyDecision { });
 
+    // Refuse to convert a navigation response into a download when the request is a data: URL,
+    // unless the navigation was driven by the API client (e.g. -loadRequest: with a data: URL).
+    // Otherwise a compromised Web Content process could navigate to a data: URL with an unshowable
+    // MIME type and rely on the navigation delegate's stock "download unshowable responses"
+    // behavior to write attacker-controlled bytes to disk without user interaction. Legitimate
+    // downloads of data: URLs go through the navigation action policy (e.g. <a href="data:..." download>)
+    // or the explicit download API, neither of which reaches this code path.
+    if (action == PolicyAction::Download && request.url().protocolIsData()
+        && (!navigation || !navigation->isFromAPIClientRequest())) {
+        WEBPAGEPROXY_RELEASE_LOG(Loading, "receivedNavigationResponsePolicyDecision: refusing to download data: URL not initiated by API client");
+        action = PolicyAction::Ignore;
+    }
+
     Ref pageLoadState = internals().pageLoadState;
     auto transaction = pageLoadState->transaction();
 
Description: CVE-2026-43658 - Fix JSC WebAssembly BBQCallee/OMGOSREntryCallee use-after-free
 In JavaScriptCore's WebAssembly tier-up path, CalleeGroup::updateCallsitesToCallUs()
 collected callsites of a caller's BBQCallee (and its owned OMGOSREntryCallee) and then
 repatched them in a later phase. Because wasm Callees are released under
 Heap::stopThePeriphery(), which only stops JS compiler threads and not wasm ones, a
 weakly-held BBQCallee and its OMGOSREntryCallee could be destroyed between callsite
 collection and repatch, leading to a use-after-free (CWE-416) that is exploitable from
 untrusted WebAssembly content.
 .
 This backports upstream fix 9a16de46f205 (WebKit bug 307669, WSA-2026-0003) to the
 2.50.6 CalleeGroup callee-storage layout. The BBQCallee is now kept alive for the
 required duration by appending it to a keep-alive vector once its callsites are
 collected; since BBQCallee owns its OMGOSREntryCallee, that also keeps the
 OMGOSREntryCallee alive. The edge case where an OMGOSREntryCallee is still weakly
 referenced while its owning BBQCallee has already been freed is handled by keeping the
 OMGOSREntryCallee alive separately, but only when no BBQCallee was retained, so the
 callee never ends up with multiple owners.
Origin: upstream, https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/commit/9a16de46f205d3c76cc16f12dca3df02705791c3
Bug: https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=307669
Author: WebKit contributors
Last-Update: 2026-09-14
---
--- a/Source/JavaScriptCore/wasm/WasmCalleeGroup.cpp
+++ b/Source/JavaScriptCore/wasm/WasmCalleeGroup.cpp
@@ -251,9 +251,18 @@ void CalleeGroup::updateCallsitesToCallUs(const AbstractLocker& locker, CodeLoca
     };

     // This is necessary since Callees are released under `Heap::stopThePeriphery()`, but that only stops JS compiler
-    // threads and not wasm ones. So the OMGOSREntryCallee could die between the time we collect the callsites and when
-    // we actually repatch its callsites.
+    // threads and not wasm ones. So a weakly held BBQCallee and its OMGOSREntryCallee could die between the time we
+    // collect the callsites and when we actually repatch its callsites. Since BBQCallee owns OMGOSREntryCallee,
+    // keeping BBQCallee alive is enough to ensure that both are alive for the required duration.
+    //
+    // There is however an edge case here - it can happen that a BBQCallee has been freed but its OMGOSREntryCallee
+    // has been added to the pending-destruction set and not yet free'd. This means that m_osrEntryCallees will still
+    // hold a weak ref to it. In this scenario, BBQCallee won't be kept alive since it does not exist so we manually
+    // have to keep the OMGOSREntryCallee alive separately. This should only be done in this scenario else we will
+    // end up with multiple owners for OMGOSREntryCallee.
+
     // FIXME: These inline capacities were picked semi-randomly. We should figure out if there's a better number.
+    Vector<RefPtr<BBQCallee>, 4> keepAliveBBQCallees;
     Vector<RefPtr<OMGOSREntryCallee>, 4> keepAliveOSREntryCallees;
     Vector<Callsite, 16> callsites;

@@ -275,12 +284,16 @@ void CalleeGroup::updateCallsitesToCallUs(const AbstractLocker& locker, CodeLoca
     auto handleCallerIndex = [&](size_t caller) {
         auto callerIndex = FunctionCodeIndex(caller);
         assertIsHeld(m_lock);
+        bool bbqCalleeKeptAlive = false;
+        UNUSED_VARIABLE(bbqCalleeKeptAlive);
 #if ENABLE(WEBASSEMBLY_BBQJIT)
         // This callee could be weak but we still need to update it since it could call our BBQ callee
         // that we're going to want to destroy.
         if (RefPtr<BBQCallee> bbqCallee = m_bbqCallees[callerIndex].get()) {
             collectCallsites(bbqCallee.get());
             ASSERT(!bbqCallee->osrEntryCallee() || m_osrEntryCallees.find(callerIndex) != m_osrEntryCallees.end());
+            keepAliveBBQCallees.append(WTFMove(bbqCallee));
+            bbqCalleeKeptAlive = true;
         }
 #endif
 #if ENABLE(WEBASSEMBLY_OMGJIT)
@@ -288,7 +301,17 @@ void CalleeGroup::updateCallsitesToCallUs(const AbstractLocker& locker, CodeLoca
         if (auto iter = m_osrEntryCallees.find(callerIndex); iter != m_osrEntryCallees.end()) {
             if (RefPtr callee = iter->value.get()) {
                 collectCallsites(callee.get());
-                keepAliveOSREntryCallees.append(WTFMove(callee));
+                // If we track the OMGOSREntryCallee as a callsite there are 2 possibilities -
+                // 1. The BBQCallee is already being tracked - in this case we don't have to
+                //    track the OMGOSREntryCallee since the BBQCallee owns it and keeping the
+                //    BBQCallee alive is good enough to keep the OMGOSREntryCallee alive. Also,
+                //    OMGOSREntryCallee is only supposed to be owned by BBQCallee
+                // 2. The BBQCallee is not tracked - This happens if the BBQCallee is already
+                //    released but the OMGOSREntryCallee is still alive. In this case there is
+                //    no other strong reference to OMGOSREntryCallee so we have to keep it
+                //    alive here.
+                if (!bbqCalleeKeptAlive)
+                    keepAliveOSREntryCallees.append(WTFMove(callee));
             } else
                 m_osrEntryCallees.remove(iter);
         }
Description: CVE-2026-20664 - Fix use-after-free / out-of-bounds write after growing a WebAssembly.Memory-backed ArrayBuffer
 Processing maliciously crafted web content may lead to an unexpected process
 crash (CWE-787, out-of-bounds write). Growing a non-shared WebAssembly.Memory
 can relocate its backing store. The wrapping ArrayBuffer caches a direct base
 pointer (ArrayBufferContents::m_data) and each typed-array view caches its own
 direct data pointer (JSArrayBufferView::m_vector) into that backing store.
 These cached pointers were not refreshed when the memory grew, so a subsequent
 access through a stale view could read or write freed memory.
 .
 This backports the upstream fix (WebKit Bugzilla 306136, commit
 6b357f32c6075bb807f52fd6cf4a68fe0ba38256, shipped in WebKitGTK 2.52.1 /
 WSA-2026-0002) to the WebKitGTK 2.50.6 tree used by Debian bookworm. Because
 2.50.6 predates the resizable-WebAssembly-buffer feature, the fix is adapted to
 this tree's grow-then-detach buffer model:
 .
   * JSArrayBufferView::refreshVector() is added to re-point a view's cached
     m_vector at a relocated backing store (guarded by hasVector()).
   * ArrayBuffer::refreshAfterWasmMemoryGrow() updates the buffer's own cached
     m_data/m_sizeInBytes and then refreshes every incoming typed-array view so
     none keep pointing at the freed region.
   * JSWebAssemblyMemory::growSuccessCallback() calls the refresh on the live,
     non-shared buffer before detaching it, closing the stale-pointer window
     without changing the spec-visible detach-on-grow behaviour.
 .
 A regression test is added at
 JSTests/wasm/stress/resizable-buffer-grow-view-refresh.js, adapted to the
 buffer semantics present in 2.50.x (the upstream test used the
 toResizableBuffer() API that does not exist in this release).
Origin: backport, https://github.com/WebKit/WebKit/commit/6b357f32c6075bb807f52fd6cf4a68fe0ba38256
Bug: https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=306136
Bug-Debian: https://security-tracker.debian.org/tracker/CVE-2026-20664
Author: Patch Platoon AI
Last-Update: 2026-09-11
---
--- /dev/null
+++ b/JSTests/wasm/stress/resizable-buffer-grow-view-refresh.js
@@ -0,0 +1,68 @@
+// Regression test for CVE-2026-20664 (WebKit Bugzilla 306136, CWE-787).
+//
+// Growing a non-shared WebAssembly.Memory can relocate its backing store. Any
+// ArrayBuffer wrapping that memory, and any typed-array view over that buffer,
+// caches a direct pointer into the (old) backing store. If those cached pointers
+// are not refreshed/invalidated when the memory grows, a subsequent access
+// through the stale view reads or writes freed memory -- an out-of-bounds
+// write / use-after-free reachable from crafted web content.
+//
+// This build of WebKitGTK (2.50.x) does not expose the resizable-buffer APIs
+// (Memory.prototype.toResizableBuffer / --useWasmMemoryToBufferAPIs) that the
+// upstream fix's test relies on, so this is a replacement test tailored to the
+// buffer semantics actually present here: on grow, the previously handed-out
+// buffer (and its views) must be detached rather than left pointing at the
+// relocated/freed store, and a freshly requested buffer must observe the grown
+// size. It also drives allocation churn after the grow so that any stale
+// pointer would land on reused memory, turning a latent UAF into an observable
+// mismatch.
+
+import * as assert from '../assert.js';
+
+// Create allocation pressure so that a relocation-on-grow is likely to move the
+// backing store to a different address, and so that any freed region is quickly
+// reused by subsequent allocations.
+const filler = [];
+for (let i = 0; i < 200; i++) {
+    try {
+        filler.push(new WebAssembly.Memory({ initial: 1, maximum: 100 }));
+    } catch (e) { break; }
+}
+
+const memory = new WebAssembly.Memory({ initial: 1, maximum: 10 });
+
+// Obtain the buffer and a typed-array view, then write a sentinel through it.
+const oldBuffer = memory.buffer;
+const oldView = new Uint32Array(oldBuffer);
+const sentinel = 0xCAFEBABE;
+oldView[0] = sentinel;
+assert.eq(oldView[0], sentinel, "sentinel should be readable before grow");
+const oldByteLength = oldBuffer.byteLength;
+
+// Grow the memory. This may relocate the backing store.
+memory.grow(1);
+
+// Churn the heap so that the old backing store, if freed, is reused. If the old
+// view/buffer still pointed at the freed region, the following reads would
+// observe attacker-controlled bytes instead of failing safely.
+for (let i = 0; i < 100; i++) {
+    const arr = new ArrayBuffer(65536);
+    new Uint32Array(arr).fill(0xDEADBEEF);
+}
+
